WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.580
A man's testes keep making sperm through adult life.

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.080
A woman's ovaries usually release one egg per cycle.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.780
What keeps the gonads on schedule?

4
00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.210
The control sits at three levels, stacked top to bottom.

5
00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.890
The hypothalamus is at the base of the brain.

6
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.510
The pituitary gland hangs just below it.

7
00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.270
Then come the gonads, the testes or ovaries.

8
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:36.910
Neurons in the hypothalamus release a hormone called GnRH, gonadotropin-releasing

9
00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.530
hormone. Portal vessels carry it down to the pituitary.

10
00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.270
And it arrives in pulses, not a steady stream.

11
00:00:46.383 --> 00:00:51.163
The pituitary answers with two hormones of its own, LH and FSH.

12
00:00:52.623 --> 00:00:54.403
They travel in the blood to the gonads.

13
00:00:55.723 --> 00:00:59.143
Because they act on gonads, they are called gonadotropins.

14
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:02.047
Follow them into a testis.

15
00:01:03.307 --> 00:01:07.307
LH reaches Leydig cells, which sit between the sperm-making tubules.

16
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:09.667
They make testosterone.

17
00:01:11.207 --> 00:01:16.467
FSH reaches Sertoli cells, which line the tubules and support the developing sperm.

18
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:19.717
Now the loop closes.

19
00:01:21.197 --> 00:01:25.957
Testosterone travels back up and lowers the output of the hypothalamus and the

20
00:01:25.957 --> 00:01:31.777
pituitary. When a product turns down its own pathway, that is negative feedback.

21
00:01:33.137 --> 00:01:34.177
Think of it as a brake.

22
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.470
With the brake on, LH and FSH fall.

23
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:44.910
When testosterone drops, the brake eases, and LH and FSH climb back up.

24
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.770
So here is a test of the loop.

25
00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.290
Remove the testes.

26
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.150
What happens to LH and FSH in the blood?

27
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:56.510
Do they go up, or down?

28
00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:09.453
Both go up. With the testes gone, their testosterone is gone, so the main brake is

29
00:02:09.453 --> 00:02:14.713
off. The pituitary keeps driving, and LH and FSH climb.

30
00:02:15.783 --> 00:02:18.363
FSH has a second brake of its own.

31
00:02:19.743 --> 00:02:23.903
Sertoli cells release a hormone called inhibin B, which lowers FSH.

32
00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:27.703
Remove the testes, and that brake goes too.

33
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:31.093
The ovary runs on the same loop.

34
00:02:32.313 --> 00:02:36.413
There the brake comes from the follicle, the sac of cells around a growing egg.

35
00:02:37.693 --> 00:02:42.113
Its hormone, estradiol, brakes the loop through most of the cycle's first half.

36
00:02:43.253 --> 00:02:47.613
But when estradiol stays high for long enough, the brake becomes an accelerator.

37
00:02:49.053 --> 00:02:51.953
After ovulation, progesterone brakes instead.

38
00:02:53.213 --> 00:02:54.413
The lesson shows how.

39
00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:56.987
One thing is still odd.

40
00:02:58.647 --> 00:03:01.847
GnRH reaches the pituitary in bursts, not a stream.

41
00:03:03.187 --> 00:03:05.907
Is the burst just how it travels, or is it the message?

42
00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:11.287
So the brain drives the gonads in pulses, through the pituitary.

43
00:03:12.387 --> 00:03:16.367
And the gonads' hormones come back up as a brake, almost all the time.
